---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const papers = await getCollection('papers');
import sn from '../data/pantheon_hubble.json';
import bao from '../data/desi_dr2_bao.json';
import tt from '../data/cmb_tt.json';
const example = papers.find((p) => p.id === 'desi-dr2-bao-2025') ?? papers[0];
const exampleJson = JSON.stringify(
  {
    title: example.data.title,
    shortName: example.data.shortName,
    authors: example.data.authors,
    year: example.data.year,
    date: example.data.date,
    arxiv: example.data.arxiv,
    doi: example.data.doi,
    journal: example.data.journal,
    probes: example.data.probes,
    summary: example.data.summary,
    status: example.data.status,
    checkedOn: example.data.checkedOn,
    results: example.data.results.slice(0, 2),
  },
  null,
  2,
);
const repo = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_REPO_URL ?? '';
---

<Base title="About — Expansion history" description="How the figures are computed and how to add a paper.">
  <article class="wrap about">
    <header>
      <h1>About this site</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A reference page for the expansion history of the universe that stays current because adding a paper is adding
        one small file.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>What is computed, and how</h2>
      <p class="measure">
        Each result supplies a set of background parameters — <i>H</i>₀, Ωₘ and, where fitted, <i>w</i>₀, <i>w</i>ₐ or
        Ω<sub>k</sub>. The site solves the Friedmann equation for that set,
      </p>
      <pre class="eq">H²(a) / H₀² = Ω<sub>r</sub> a⁻⁴ + Ωₘ a⁻³ + Ω<sub>k</sub> a⁻² + Ω<sub>de</sub> · a^(−3(1+w₀+wₐ)) · e^(3wₐ(a−1))</pre>
      <p class="measure">
        with <i>w</i>(<i>a</i>) = <i>w</i>₀ + <i>w</i>ₐ(1 − <i>a</i>), radiation from <i>T</i><sub>CMB</sub> = 2.7255 K and
        <i>N</i><sub>eff</sub> = 3.044, and Ω<sub>de</sub> fixed by flatness unless Ω<sub>k</sub> is given. Cosmic time,
        lookback time and the comoving, luminosity and angular-diameter distances follow by quadrature. The implementation
        (<code>src/lib/cosmology.ts</code>) is tested against astropy to better than 0.03 % at every redshift the tests
        cover, for ΛCDM, <i>w</i>CDM, <i>w</i>₀<i>w</i>ₐCDM and open models.
      </p>
      <p class="measure">
        Nothing is fitted here. Only parameter values and their quoted 68 % intervals are copied from the papers; the
        figures show those values and the histories they imply. Correlations between parameters are not stored, so the
        error bars in the <i>w</i>₀–<i>w</i>ₐ plane are marginal intervals, not contours.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="measurements">
      <h2>Measurements drawn directly</h2>
      <p class="measure">
        Three chapters of the home-page story plot data rather than fitted parameters. The files are generated by scripts
        in <code>scripts/</code> and committed in <code>src/data/</code>, so every point can be traced to its release.
      </p>
      <ul class="measure">
        <li>
          <b>Supernovae.</b> {sn.count.toLocaleString('en-US')} Type Ia supernovae from the Pantheon+ compilation (z &gt; 0.01,
          Cepheid calibrators excluded, repeat light curves of one supernova averaged), shown as distance modulus relative
          to an empty universe. The supernova absolute magnitude is a free offset, fitted to Planck 2018 ΛCDM. Bars are
          inverse-variance means in {sn.bins.length} redshift bins using the diagonal errors only.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Expansion speed.</b> DESI DR2 BAO <i>D</i><sub>H</sub>/<i>r</i><sub>d</sub> at six redshifts, converted to
          <i>a</i>·<i>H</i>(<i>z</i>)/<i>H</i>₀ with the Planck 2018 sound horizon (<i>r</i><sub>d</sub> = {bao.rd} Mpc) and
          <i>H</i>₀ = {bao.H0}. The conversion borrows Planck's ruler, so the points test the shape of the history, not its scale.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>First light.</b> The spectrum is CAMB's Planck 2018 best fit (first peak at ℓ = {tt.peaks[0]}). The sky on the
          sphere and in the panel is one random sky drawn from that spectrum with healpy — a simulation with the right
          statistics, not the Planck map.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="add">
      <h2>Adding or correcting a paper</h2>
      <ol class="measure steps">
        <li>
          Create <code>data/papers/&lt;short-id&gt;.json</code> (for example <code>desi-dr3-bao-2027.json</code>). The
          build validates it against the schema in <code>src/content.config.ts</code> and refuses anything malformed.
        </li>
        <li>
          Put every fit you want shown in <code>results</code>. A result with <code>H0</code> and <code>Om</code> can be
          drawn as an expansion history; one with <code>w0</code> and <code>wa</code> appears in the dark-energy plane; one
          with only <code>H0</code> appears in the <i>H</i>₀ ladder. Mark up to three results <code>"featured": true</code>
          to show them by default.
        </li>
        <li>
          Say where each number is in <code>source</code> (<code>"abstract"</code>, <code>"Table V"</code>,
          <code>"eq. (28)"</code>). Leave <code>"status": "unverified"</code> until you have compared every number with
          the paper itself, then switch it to <code>"verified"</code> and add <code>"checkedOn"</code>. Use
          <code>notes</code> for anything a reader should know (which likelihood, derived quantities, version changes).
        </li>
        <li>Open a pull request. The deploy workflow rebuilds the site on merge.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="measure">A trimmed example (the first two results of the DESI DR2 BAO entry):</p>
      <pre><code>{exampleJson}</code></pre>
      <p class="measure">
        Fields: <code>probes</code> is one or more of <code>CMB, CMBlens, BAO, Lya, SN, WL, GC, Clusters, CC, Ladder, TD,
        GW, BBN</code>; <code>model</code> is one of <code>LCDM, wCDM, w0waCDM, oLCDM, ow0waCDM</code>; each parameter is
        <code>{'{ "value", "sigma" }'}</code> or <code>{'{ "value", "plus", "minus" }'}</code> with an optional
        <code>note</code>.
      </p>
      <p class="measure">
        A weekly workflow (<code>.github/workflows/arxiv-watch.yml</code>) searches arXiv astro-ph.CO for new
        expansion-history and dark-energy papers and opens an issue listing candidates not yet in
        <code>data/papers/</code>. <code>python3 scripts/new_paper.py &lt;arxiv-id&gt;</code> drafts the JSON skeleton from
        the arXiv metadata so only the numbers remain to be filled in.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Reading the figures</h2>
      <ul class="measure">
        <li>Lines are published fits; the dashed line is whatever you dial in with the sliders.</li>
        <li>Error bars are the 68 % intervals quoted in the abstracts or tables; asymmetric ones are drawn as such.</li>
        <li>Each figure has a table view with the same numbers, and every value links back to its arXiv record.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    {repo && (
      <section>
        <h2>Source</h2>
        <p class="measure"><a href={repo} rel="noopener">{repo}</a></p>
      </section>
    )}
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .about {
    display: grid;
    gap: 2.5rem;
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.5rem) clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem);
  }
  .about header { display: grid; gap: 0.9rem; }
  .about section { display: grid; gap: 0.9rem; }
  .eq { font-size: 0.95rem; max-width: var(--measure); }
  .steps li { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  pre code { white-space: pre; }
</style>
